\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related_work}

\subsection{VLA Robustness}

\looseness=-1 Vision-language-action models have progressed from tokenized action prediction~\cite{brohan2023rt2,kim2024openvla,ghosh2024octo} to generative approaches based on diffusion~\cite{chi2023diffusion_policy} and flow matching~\cite{black2024pi0,shukor2025smolvla}. Generative VLAs produce stochastic action samples that enable multi-sample inference; tokenized VLAs, by contrast, yield near-identical outputs across draws and cannot support sample-based refinement. Robustness evaluations~\cite{fei2025liberoplus} show that current VLAs lose over 65 percentage points under common deployment perturbations, motivating both training-time and inference-time defenses.

\paragraph{Training-time methods.}
\looseness=-1 Recent approaches improve robustness through modified training. SRPO~\cite{srpo2025} and RobustVLA~\cite{robustvla2025} apply reinforcement learning post-training: the former uses the policy's own rollouts as reward references, while the latter adds Jacobian and smoothness regularization for stability under observation noise. DiG-Flow~\cite{digflow2025} operates at the representation level, gating control signals via distributional discrepancy between observation and action embeddings. TT-VLA~\cite{liu2026ttvla} adapts parameters at test time through progress-reward RL but requires per-task reward design. All four methods require retraining, reward engineering, or environment interaction and cannot serve as drop-in improvements to a frozen VLA.

\paragraph{Inference-time methods.}
ReconVLA~\cite{chen2026reconvla} applies conformal quantile regression to calibrate per-action uncertainty bounds but does not learn corrective residuals. SC-VLA~\cite{liu2026scvla} performs online refinement through sparse world imagination combined with residual RL, but still requires task-level RL training. VLA-Pilot~\cite{li2025vlapilot} uses evolutionary diffusion optimization guided by an MLLM verifier at 2.41\,s per control step.

\looseness=-1 A2C2~\cite{a2c2_2025} is the closest concurrent work. It learns a lightweight correction head that adjusts each action within a pre-generated chunk at every control step, addressing \emph{temporal staleness}: as new observations arrive mid-chunk, A2C2 corrects actions planned under stale context. Our pipeline targets a complementary problem, \emph{initial action quality}, by improving the action before execution. The two approaches could in principle be composed. Our pipeline differs in three ways: (1)~multi-sample selection precedes correction and centers the initial candidate; (2)~explicit $L_2$ norm clipping controls correction magnitude (Section~\ref{sec:magnitude_results}); and (3)~the correction module conditions on the frozen VLA's observation embedding rather than temporal action features.

\subsection{Residual Policy Learning}

\looseness=-1 Residual policy learning augments a base controller with a learned additive correction~\cite{silver2018residual,johannink2019residual}. The central challenge is magnitude control: unconstrained residuals can dominate the base policy and effectively learn from scratch. TRANSIC~\cite{jiang2024transic} mitigates this with a learned binary gate that disables correction when the base policy suffices; without the gate, performance collapses. PLD~\cite{xiao2026pld} bounds corrections within $[-\xi, \xi]$ via off-policy RL, and DRC~\cite{pan2025drc} applies exponential decay to correction magnitude over training. All three rely on RL and environment interaction. Our correction module uses fixed $L_2$ norm clipping with supervised $L_2$ loss on demonstration data. It requires no RL, no reward design, and no online rollouts.

\subsection{Inference-Time Compute Scaling}

Test-time compute scaling in language models improves output quality by generating multiple candidates and selecting among them~\cite{snell2024scaling}. RoVer~\cite{dai2025rover} transfers this principle to VLAs with a reward model that scores candidates in a best-of-N scheme. DA-SIP~\cite{chun2025dasip} adapts the number of denoising steps based on per-step difficulty rather than using a fixed budget.

\looseness=-1 In the language domain, selection among candidates is the primary mechanism for quality improvement. Our decomposition reveals a different pattern in low-dimensional continuous action spaces: learned correction accounts for 78\% of the gain at $K{=}10$ and 55\% at $K{=}5$ (Section~\ref{sec:main_results}), while multi-sample selection provides centering but not the dominant contribution. This asymmetry motivates per-step adaptive allocation: a cheap $K{=}2$ uncertainty probe ($\rho{=}0.9979$ vs.\ $K{=}10$ variance) determines whether to extend sampling and apply correction, achieving 91\% of fixed-$K{=}10$ performance at 38\% of the compute (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k}). Unlike DA-SIP, which adapts denoising steps within a diffusion policy, our probe operates in action space and triggers a qualitatively different computation path (selection + learned correction) rather than adjusting iteration count.
Conformal prediction~\cite{angelopoulos2023gentle} has been applied to robotics for safety constraints~\cite{zhou2025acp_cbf,wang2025cploop} and teleoperation calibration~\cite{zhao2024conformalized}; we test CP scores as correction features and find they do not improve over observation-only conditioning (Section~\ref{sec:magnitude_results}).

